A||||2||Taijiguy||REAL wood interior trim||||1||
Z||000000||Taijiguy||07-22-2000||08:54 AM||||Maybe this shoud be posted in the mall, but I figured I'd cover my bases.<BR>Once upon a time, in another life, I used to be a trim carpenter. While in the process of my current interior mods, I realized I have the tools and knowledge to make exact copies of the Fiero facias in real wood.<BR>My question is, how many would be interested in owning real wood facias? Prices would vary depending on the wood itself. Most popular types would be oak, maple, and maybe cherry.  Cheapest would be poplar. They're all hardwoods, and I could offer them in any stage of readiness. The advantage of the real wood, it ocurred to me, is it could be stained any color, to match the color of your interior! Also, you could have your choice of finishes, flat, satin, semi-gloss, or gloss. I would anticipate the prices to vary from maybe 50-75 bucks for unfinished blank overlays, to maybe 300 or 400 for complete ready to install facias (NOT overlays) (Unless you want some super exotic wood, then price would be commisurate)<BR>Any feedback about this? Is anyone doing this already?<P>Later,<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||07-22-2000||10:31 AM|||| <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/gtfieroracer/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/gtfieroracer/</A> <P>is making them with veneers that would stick on the dash plates.  I like the wood look.  My only concern is their durability and my horse hand skills at putting them on correctly.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||07-22-2000||08:07 PM||||The ones I'm making are real wood, not a veneer. They would actually replace the existing panels, not glue to them. I think it provides a richer look. And being able to finish them as you want is a benefit as well.<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
